# knapsack.zd - what to take, when the bag is too small for everything.
#
# Eight things, each with a weight and a worth, and a bag that holds
# twenty-one. Which subset is worth the most? There are 256 subsets here,
# and the greedy answer, taking the best worth-per-weight first, is wrong
# at this size: it reaches 39, and the best is 42. Greedy takes the map
# and the camera, which are cheap and look good per unit weight, and then
# has two units of room left and a sandwich that needs seven. The right
# answer leaves the map and the camera behind and carries the sandwich.
# The program below is the 0/1 knapsack recurrence, which finds it.
#
# Make the bag bigger and smaller, and drop things from the pile, and the
# answer is recomputed from nothing on every click. That is the signal
# graph: `picked` depends on `table`, `table` on `available` and `room`,
# `available` on `dropped`, and none of those dependencies is written
# anywhere.
#
# WHAT IS INTERESTING ABOUT THE ANSWER. Dropping one thing does not just
# remove one line from the answer; it rearranges the rest. Leave the
# sandwich behind and the map and the camera come back into the bag, both
# of which the best answer had refused to carry a moment earlier. That is
# visible by clicking, which is the reason this example has buttons at
# all: a knapsack that printed one number would prove nothing a comment
# could not have claimed.
#
# THE SAME TABLE PROBLEM AS `edit-distance.zd`, AND NOT REPEATED HERE.
# This is a two-dimensional dynamic program and there is no
# two-dimensional structure, so the table is a flat `List of Whole` with
# `(row * width) + col` written out by hand, grown a row at a time with
# `flatten`. #195 asked for `setAt` and `setAt` is in the prelude now,
# which does not change this: it copies the list it writes into, as a
# `Map` insert copies the table. The cost is the copy, not the spelling,
# and the copy is #233. What is worth adding is that here the table's
# *width* is a signal: the bag's size is on a
# button, so the whole table is a different shape after every click, and
# nothing about the flat representation makes that worse than a nested one
# would be. It is rebuilt either way.
#
# WHAT IS NEW HERE. Reading the answer back out. The table holds what the
# best worth *is* and not what to take, so `chosenFrom` walks back up the
# rows asking, at each one, whether this row's answer differs from the row
# above it: if it does, this item was taken. That is the DP traceback, it
# is a fold with the answer as a parameter like everything else, and it is
# the reason the page can show a list of things rather than a number.

record Item
    id     is Whole
    name   is Text
    weight is Whole
    worth  is Whole

# A collection literal may not span lines.
state goods is client List of Item starting [(Item with id is 0, name is "map", weight is 2, worth is 3), (Item with id is 1, name is "compass", weight is 2, worth is 4), (Item with id is 2, name is "water", weight is 6, worth is 10), (Item with id is 3, name is "sandwich", weight is 7, worth is 11), (Item with id is 4, name is "glucose", weight is 1, worth is 1), (Item with id is 5, name is "banana", weight is 4, worth is 9), (Item with id is 6, name is "suntan cream", weight is 1, worth is 7), (Item with id is 7, name is "camera", weight is 3, worth is 5)]

# What the user has taken out of the pile, by id. A list rather than a
# `Truth` per item, because `Checkbox` binds one named signal and there is
# no way to make one per element of a collection.
state dropped is client List of Whole starting empty

# The bag. Clamped rather than guarded: a negative capacity would make the
# table a negative number of cells wide and the arithmetic would go
# quietly wrong instead of loudly.
state capacity is client Whole starting 21
state room is client Whole from clamp with value is capacity, low is 0, high is 30

state available is client List of Item from keptItems with goods is goods, dropped is dropped

function keptItems with goods, dropped
    from goods
    keep each item where not (dropped contains item.id)

# `remove` and `append` are the two mutations, and which one to run is a
# question about the list, so it is answered in a function and the handler
# is a `set`.
function toggledIn with chosen, id
    if chosen contains id
        give withoutIt with chosen is chosen, id is id
    give append id to chosen

function withoutIt with chosen, id
    from chosen
    keep each held where held is not id

function followedBy with before, after
    give flatten of [before, after]

function cellAt with table, width, row, col
    give valueOr with maybe is (listAt with value is table, index is (row * width) + col), fallback is 0

# --- the recurrence -------------------------------------------------------

# `table[row][load]` is the best worth reachable using the first `row`
# things without exceeding `load`. Row zero is all zeros: nothing to take
# is worth nothing at every capacity.
function bestFor with items, room
    give bestRows with items is items, width is room + 1, row is 1, rows is (length of items) + 1, table is (filled with value is 0, count is room + 1)

function bestRows with items, width, row, rows, table
    if row >= rows
        give table
    give bestRows with items is items, width is width, row is row + 1, rows is rows, table is (followedBy with before is table, after is (bestRow with table is table, items is items, width is width, row is row, load is 0, made is empty))

function bestRow with table, items, width, row, load, made
    if load >= width
        give made
    give bestRow with table is table, items is items, width is width, row is row, load is load + 1, made is (append (bestCell with table is table, items is items, width is width, row is row, load is load) to made)

# Take it or leave it, and keep whichever is worth more. The whole of the
# algorithm is these six lines; everything else in this file is the table
# the language does not provide.
function bestCell with table, items, width, row, load
    with without is (cellAt with table is table, width is width, row is row - 1, col is load)
    when listAt with value is items, index is row - 1
        None
            give without
        Some with item
            if item.weight > load
                give without
            give max with first is without, second is ((cellAt with table is table, width is width, row is row - 1, col is load - item.weight) + item.worth)

# --- reading the choice back out ------------------------------------------

# The traceback. A row whose answer is the same as the row above it did
# not take its item; a row whose answer differs did, and the capacity it
# was taken out of is this row's `load` less its weight.
function chosenFrom with table, items, width, row, load, taken
    if row < 1
        give taken
    if (cellAt with table is table, width is width, row is row, col is load) is (cellAt with table is table, width is width, row is row - 1, col is load)
        give chosenFrom with table is table, items is items, width is width, row is row - 1, load is load, taken is taken
    when listAt with value is items, index is row - 1
        None
            give taken
        Some with item
            give chosenFrom with table is table, items is items, width is width, row is row - 1, load is load - item.weight, taken is (append item to taken)

function packed with table, items, width, room
    give reverse of (chosenFrom with table is table, items is items, width is width, row is (length of items), load is room, taken is empty)

# --- the greedy answer, for contrast --------------------------------------

# Best worth per weight first, take it if it fits. It is what a person
# does and it is wrong here, which is the point of showing it.
#
# The ratio is scaled by 100 and floored because `sort each … by` orders
# by a key and a `Decimal` key would sort fine but read badly; the
# ordering is the same either way. It is negated because `sort each` is
# ascending and there is no `sort each … descending`, and no comparator
# can be passed to make one (§17.2.5).
function byValueDensity of items
    from items
    sort each item by 0 - (density of item)

function density of item
    give valueOr with maybe is (floor of ((decimalOf of (item.worth * 100)) / (decimalOf of item.weight))), fallback is 0

function greedyWorth with items, room, index, worth, used
    when listAt with value is items, index is index
        None
            give worth
        Some with item
            if used + item.weight > room
                give greedyWorth with items is items, room is room, index is index + 1, worth is worth, used is used
            give greedyWorth with items is items, room is room, index is index + 1, worth is worth + item.worth, used is used + item.weight

# --- what the page shows -------------------------------------------------

state width is client Whole from room + 1
state table is client List of Whole from bestFor with items is available, room is room
state best  is client Whole from cellAt with table is table, width is width, row is (length of available), col is room

state picked      is client List of Item from packed with table is table, items is available, width is width, room is room
state packedWeight is client Whole from sumOf of (weightsOf of picked)
state greedy      is client Whole from greedyWorth with items is (byValueDensity of available), room is room, index is 0, worth is 0, used is 0

function weightsOf of items
    from items
    map each item to item.weight

view
    Column
        Heading "What to take"
        Text "Eight things, each with a weight and a worth. The bag holds what it holds. Change the bag, or leave something behind, and the answer is worked out again."

        Row
            Text "the bag holds "
            Text room, weight is "bold"
            Button "bigger"
                on click
                    add 1 to capacity
            Button "smaller"
                on click
                    subtract 1 from capacity

        Column padding is 8
            Heading "the pile"
            each item in goods
                Row gap is 8
                    if dropped contains item.id
                        Text item.name, decoration is "struck", color is "grey"
                    otherwise
                        Text item.name
                    Text "weighs "
                    Text item.weight
                    Text "worth "
                    Text item.worth
                    Button "leave it"
                        on click
                            set dropped to toggledIn with chosen is dropped, id is item.id

        Column padding is 8
            Heading "take these"
            each item in picked
                Row gap is 8
                    Text item.name, weight is "bold"
                    Text item.weight
                    Text item.worth
            Row
                Text "worth "
                Text best, weight is "bold", size is "large"
                Text " and weighs "
                Text packedWeight
                Text " of "
                Text room

        Column padding is 8
            Heading "what greedy would have done"
            Row
                Text "best worth per weight first, taking what fits: "
                Text greedy, weight is "bold"
            if greedy < best
                Text "Short, and by how much is the whole reason this program is a table and not a loop.", color is "grey"
            otherwise
                Text "The same answer this time. It is not the same answer in general, and the default bag is a case where it is not.", color is "grey"
